<!--

    Copyright (C) 2026 Antony Stubbs and contributors

    THE .NET DEMO (astubbs#242, plan unit U35). The same records through .NET's own Kafka client,
    one at a time, and through this module's client library over a real sidecar.

    IN THE MODULE'S ONE SOLUTION, not beside it. Two reasons, and the first is a rule this module
    already carries: `dotnet build`, `dotnet test` and the CI row's `dotnet format analyzers` all
    run here with NO project argument and error out if the module root holds more than one
    candidate, so a demo project has to join the existing solution rather than add a second one.
    The second is that a demo nobody compiles rots - being in the solution means the module's
    ordinary build, and therefore the clients CI row, keeps this compiling.

    IT TARGETS net8.0 LIKE THE REST OF THE MODULE, and rolls forward like the conformance runner,
    for the same measured reason: `dotnet run` launches a real net8.0 app, and on a machine whose
    only runtime is newer that dies with "You must install or update .NET" unless it may roll
    forward. The demo is launched directly by demo/run.sh, so it meets that difference exactly as
    the conformance runner does.

-->
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <AssemblyName>pc-dotnet-demo</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>Bz.Stub.ParallelConsumer.Proxy.Client.Demo</RootNamespace>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <!-- Nothing consumes this as a library, so XML docs would be documentation for nobody. -->
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <RollForward>Major</RollForward>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
        THE AK CORE ARM'S KAFKA CLIENT. Confluent.Kafka is .NET's Kafka client the way
        `org.apache.kafka:kafka-clients` is Java's - it wraps librdkafka and carries the native
        binaries for every platform this demo runs on, macOS arm64 and Debian x86_64 included.

        It is a DEMO dependency and deliberately not the library's: an application that reaches the
        engine through the sidecar needs no Kafka client at all, which is the whole point of the
        sidecar arm. Only the comparison needs one.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Confluent.Kafka" Version="2.15.0" />

    <!--
        The broker, when the caller supplies none. Same choice the Java demo makes, one language
        over: a real broker in a container is what a user actually runs.

        NEVER REACHED FROM INSIDE THE DEMO'S OWN CONTAINER - that path is always given the address
        of a compose sibling, because a demo container is never granted the host Docker socket
        (plan unit U35). Testcontainers needs that socket, so the rule and the dependency never
        meet.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Testcontainers.Kafka" Version="4.14.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/Bz.Stub.ParallelConsumer.Proxy.Client/Bz.Stub.ParallelConsumer.Proxy.Client.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Shared with the test project by link rather than by assembly; see the file's own remarks. -->
    <Compile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../../shared/JvmToolchain.cs" Link="JvmToolchain.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
